  "query_preview": "..\n\nSYNOPTIC SETUP:\nThe low pressure system that brought rain showers this morning has\nbegun to exit northeastward. Low-level moisture associated with\nthe backside of the low will continue moving throughout the\nregion, producing continued showers north of the Missouri River\nthrough this evening. Additional rain totals for the remainder of\ntoday and tonight are expected to be less than 0.20\".\n\nA tight pressure gradient on the backside of the low will result \nin strong W-NW surface winds this afternoon and evening. Sustained\nwinds will be 20 to 30 mph, while gusts have the potential to\nreach 60 mph. It should be noted that significant cloud ceilings\nmay be working to inhibit mixing, potentially diminishing chances\nfor winds as strong as the forecasted maximum. A High Wind \nWarning has been issued for eastern portions of the CWA. A Lake \nWind Advisory has been issued for Fort Peck Lake. Wave heights \nhave the potential to reach up to 6 feet.\n\nThis system will move NE and out of the region by tomorrow \nmorning as a weak upper-level ridge begins to build over Montana.\nHowever, a swath of moisture moving throughout the 700mb flow will\ncontribute to chances of rain showers across NW portions of the \nCWA early morning Wednesday. There is little indication that any \nrain amounts will exceed that of a wetting rain (0.10\" of \naccumulation), aside from limited areas straddling the Canadian \nborder.\n\nCome Thursday night, a cut off low over the California-Oregon\nborder will begin to rejoin mainstream flow and move over Idaho\nand western Montana by Friday morning. The low will then \ngradually meander across the state through the weekend. Low-level\nmoisture and vorticity-induced instability associated with the\nsystem will likely cause rain showers and potential thunderstorms\nacross the entirety of the CWA. Precipitation is currently\nanticipated to begin early Friday morning and last through\nSaturday night. As of the latest run, the NBM indicated rain\ntotals for the event of roughly .50\" with localized areas of 1\" \nor greater across the CWA. No particularly strong winds are \nanticipated for this event.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Confidence in additional rain amounts for the remainder of today\n  and tonight are moderate to high.\n\n- Confidence in rain chances and totals Wednesday morning is low.\n\n- Confidence in rain chances this weekend is moderate. Confidence\n  in rain totals this weekend is low.\n\nDON",
